  "operation": {
    "name": "lowercase hexadecimal encoding (full-length form, F1's operation)",
    "contract": "out[2*i] = table[in[i] >> 4], out[2*i+1] = table[in[i] & 0xf] for table = \"0123456789abcdef\", applied to every input byte: (length & ~0xf) bytes in the vector loop and (length & 0xf) in the scalar tail; exact for every length; input rdi, length rsi, output rdx; no return value",
    "evidence": [
      "the body's only external references are the same two constants the F1 encoder reads: a 16x0x0f nibble mask and the 16-byte ASCII table \"0123456789abcdef\" in .rodata",
      "the entry chunking is F1's, not F4's: the vector byte count is length & 0x7ffffffffffffff0 (all but the last 15 bytes), so the conversion covers the whole input at any length -- no modulo truncation, unlike F4's length & 0x3f",
      "the vector loop is the F1 loop's shape and size (17 instructions): it splits each 16-byte block into nibbles, shuffles both nibble planes through the table with pshufb, interleaves the characters and stores 32 bytes per 16 input bytes; only the counter register differs (r8, where F1 uses rcx)",
      "what F3 changes is remainder bookkeeping: the remainder length & 0xf is computed at entry, in ecx, before the loop (F1 derives it in its tail); the tail's pairs loop addresses stores through a pointer built as output base + output + 3 with displacements -3..0; and a remainder of exactly 1 shortcuts straight into the final-byte code with its index zeroed",
      "the scalar tail converts the remainder with the same table, hi nibble first: a two-byte loop over remainder & 0xe whose output base is derived as (2 * length) & ~0x1f = 2 * (length & ~0xf), plus one final byte when the remainder is odd",
      "both selecting consumers use it as the general encoder: one passes the length constant 32, the other passes a fully runtime length and allocates 2 * length for the output"
    ]
  },
